{
  "result": "I wake in the soft light of early morning, when the house is quiet and the day is still unwritten. The ritual of brewing tea has become a meditation, a small ceremony that anchors me before the world rushes in. I fill the kettle with cold water, listening to the sound as it cascades and pools. There is a certain satisfaction in choosing leaves from jars, each with its own scent, its own promise. Steam rises in a whisper once the water boils, curling in the air like a ghost of sleep leaving the room. I pour, watching as the liquid darkens, as aroma blossoms. The first sip is always the best: warm, earthy, a reminder that some of life's pleasures are found in slowing down. Outside, birds begin to announce the sun, and I find myself pausing to listen. There is a timelessness in these minutes, a sense that existence is made up of little moments stitched together. Later, emails will buzz, traffic will hum, demands will stack like cups in the sink. But now, with hands around a warm mug, I breathe and simply am. The sun inches higher, painting the kitchen in gold, and I am grateful for the ordinary grace of beginnings. In these quiet rituals, I rediscover myself daily, drawing strength from routine and beauty. There is no hurry, no expectation beyond being present. Perhaps that is why I cherish mornings, why the simple act of steeping leaves feels like an invitation to live slowly.",
